1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's one of the world's most affordable sources of protein.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:08,000
Hens lay $80 billion a year in the US alone.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
You're looking at a bird that's producing about an egg every day

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
or every other day.

5
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Grade A, double A, cage-free, free-range, pasture-raised,

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:23,000
organic, omega-3, white, brown, green, purple, black, large,

7
00:00:23,001 --> 00:00:27,000
extra-large, incredibly large, incredibly tiny, powdered,

8
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:33,000
powdered and liquid, bagged, bottled, and in tankards.

9
00:00:33,001 --> 00:00:34,800
They're lean, mean egg-miring machines.

10
00:00:34,801 --> 00:00:36,500
They're just great.

11
00:00:36,501 --> 00:00:39,440
If we cook them right, chug them wrong,

12
00:00:39,441 --> 00:00:45,120
or even sculpt their shells, they're incredible and edible.

13
00:00:45,121 --> 00:00:48,120
Now, the egg on Modern Marvels.

14
00:00:57,000 --> 00:01:03,240
Eggs, the reproductive cells from which all life forms

15
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:05,880
evolve, including us.

16
00:01:05,980 --> 00:01:10,240
So which came first, the chicken or the egg?

17
00:01:10,340 --> 00:01:13,820
The first eggs were laid by sea creatures in the ocean hundreds

18
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,280
of millions of years ago.

19
00:01:16,380 --> 00:01:19,520
In water, the eggs needed no shells for protection.

20
00:01:19,620 --> 00:01:24,060
With reptiles came soft, leathery sacks.

21
00:01:24,061 --> 00:01:28,300
Dinosaurs laid the first hard-shelled eggs.

22
00:01:28,400 --> 00:01:31,100
Later, but still 100 million years ago,

23
00:01:31,200 --> 00:01:35,180
the first birds laid their eggs in nests.

24
00:01:35,280 --> 00:01:40,300
So the egg wins because Gallus domesticus, the chicken as we

25
00:01:40,400 --> 00:01:45,180
know it, is only 4,000 or 5,000 years old.

26
00:01:45,280 --> 00:01:48,180
Today, its eggs are a major global food source.

27
00:01:51,480 --> 00:01:54,060
Welcome to the egg capital of America.

28
00:01:54,061 --> 00:01:59,900
Iowa, where every year 50 million hens lay more than 12 billion

29
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:00,700
eggs.

30
00:02:03,800 --> 00:02:08,300
In the entire US, there are as many ladybirds as people,

31
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:10,820
280 million.

32
00:02:10,920 --> 00:02:13,720
Coincidentally, each American eats about the same number of

33
00:02:13,820 --> 00:02:17,920
eggs a single chicken puts out, 250 per year.

34
00:02:18,020 --> 00:02:24,060
So in effect, every person has a hen with his or her name on it.

35
00:02:24,061 --> 00:02:28,000
There are more than 200 breeds of chickens.

36
00:02:28,100 --> 00:02:31,560
But the classic white leghorn, originally bred in Italy,

37
00:02:31,660 --> 00:02:34,300
is the choice for most egg producers.

38
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:37,040
Too small to be considered a meaty bird,

39
00:02:37,140 --> 00:02:40,300
it's built to lay eggs.

40
00:02:40,400 --> 00:02:43,120
This mega chicken coop in Stewart, Iowa,

41
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:46,920
is part of Rose Acre Farms, the nation's second largest egg

42
00:02:47,020 --> 00:02:48,320
producer.

43
00:02:48,420 --> 00:02:52,120
Every day, 1.5 million chickens housed here

44
00:02:52,121 --> 00:02:55,820
lay more than 800,000 eggs.

45
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:58,620
The process begins at hatcheries.

46
00:02:58,720 --> 00:03:02,060
Because leghorn hens are cannibalistic and may literally

47
00:03:02,160 --> 00:03:04,660
peck each other to death in close quarters,

48
00:03:04,760 --> 00:03:07,960
the sharp tips of their beaks are trimmed by machine within two

49
00:03:08,060 --> 00:03:12,680
days of birth, a process which egg producers say is painless,

50
00:03:12,780 --> 00:03:16,120
similar to trimming a fingernail.

51
00:03:16,220 --> 00:03:18,420
Because these leghorns are intended solely for egg

52
00:03:18,520 --> 00:03:21,280
production, male chicks are killed within a day or two

53
00:03:21,281 --> 00:03:24,820
or two of hatching, using procedures approved by the

54
00:03:24,920 --> 00:03:26,580
American Medical Veterinary Association.

55
00:03:29,720 --> 00:03:32,420
At an age of approximately 18 weeks,

56
00:03:32,520 --> 00:03:35,420
hens are ready to lay and are moved into production houses.

57
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:43,040
We're in house three right now, which we call 3A and B.

58
00:03:43,140 --> 00:03:46,540
A stands for attic, B stands for basement.

59
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:51,280
This case system here is eight high with a floor in the middle.

60
00:03:51,281 --> 00:03:55,360
A typical hen house at Rose Acre is built around 400-foot

61
00:03:55,460 --> 00:03:59,360
rows, with cages stacked eight high on each side.

62
00:03:59,460 --> 00:04:05,260
Every building has eight rows, housing 200 to 250,000 hens.

63
00:04:05,360 --> 00:04:08,860
The Stewart, Iowa facility has six houses, accounting for the

64
00:04:08,960 --> 00:04:14,700
total chicken population of up to 1.5 million.

65
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:18,780
Each cage houses five to seven chickens.

66
00:04:18,781 --> 00:04:22,580
The current industry guideline requires a minimum of 67 square

67
00:04:22,680 --> 00:04:27,980
inches of space per bird, about half a square foot.

68
00:04:28,080 --> 00:04:31,620
Feed is continually replenished in front of cages, water in the

69
00:04:31,720 --> 00:04:35,540
back, and manure is removed by large conveyor belts every sixth

70
00:04:35,640 --> 00:04:37,640
day.

71
00:04:37,740 --> 00:04:41,640
Eggs roll gently onto smaller belts.

72
00:04:41,740 --> 00:04:45,580
Once the egg rolls out onto the four-inch belt, the egg gets

73
00:04:45,581 --> 00:04:48,480
The egg gets rolled into the basket here.

74
00:04:48,580 --> 00:04:51,380
The basket lifts the egg up and carries it down

75
00:04:51,480 --> 00:04:53,080
to our main belt.

76
00:04:53,180 --> 00:04:55,380
On the main belt is a mass counter,

77
00:04:55,480 --> 00:04:59,020
which counts all the eggs coming out of this row.

78
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:01,620
With freezing winter temperatures in Iowa,

79
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:05,540
buildings are enclosed and use artificial light 16 to 17

80
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:07,240
hours per day.

81
00:05:07,340 --> 00:05:11,280
Chickens do sleep, about eight hours per night.

82
00:05:11,380 --> 00:05:14,880
The caged system accounts for nearly 98% of American egg

83
00:05:14,881 --> 00:05:16,480
production.

84
00:05:16,580 --> 00:05:19,280
Most eggs sold for $2 per dozen or less

85
00:05:19,380 --> 00:05:21,180
are produced this way.

86
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:24,120
An amazingly efficient process, especially when you consider

87
00:05:24,220 --> 00:05:27,920
where each egg started in the first place.

88
00:05:28,020 --> 00:05:30,120
In this cutaway of the hen, up here,

89
00:05:30,220 --> 00:05:33,440
we'd be looking at the ovary, the one functional ovary

90
00:05:33,540 --> 00:05:36,040
on the left-hand side of the hen's body.

91
00:05:36,140 --> 00:05:40,080
And this contains all the future egg yolks going from those

92
00:05:40,180 --> 00:05:43,740
that are microscopic in size to the next one that's going

93
00:05:43,741 --> 00:05:45,280
released.

94
00:05:45,380 --> 00:05:47,940
Inside a hen's oviduct, each yolk

95
00:05:48,040 --> 00:05:50,240
is covered with a membrane, fibers,

96
00:05:50,340 --> 00:05:52,980
and the egg white called albumin.

97
00:05:53,080 --> 00:05:55,720
The yolk rotates as it develops, twisting fibers

98
00:05:55,820 --> 00:05:59,120
into rope-like strands that anchor the yolk.

99
00:05:59,220 --> 00:06:01,460
The strands form the white stringy substance,

100
00:06:01,560 --> 00:06:05,420
often seen after cracking a fresh egg.

101
00:06:05,520 --> 00:06:08,740
The egg shell, a crystalline form of calcium carbonate,

102
00:06:08,840 --> 00:06:11,440
is deposited around the egg just before it's laid.

103
00:06:13,740 --> 00:06:18,820
At Rose Acre Farms, when eggs leave the hen houses, they travel into grading facilities

104
00:06:18,821 --> 00:06:21,760
where they're inspected, sized, and prepared for market.

105
00:06:23,260 --> 00:06:29,380
After passing through an automated egg wash, it's time for QC, quality control.

106
00:06:30,420 --> 00:06:33,960
What that person is doing is looking for the bad eggs.

107
00:06:34,300 --> 00:06:41,040
Bad eggs meaning bloods, leakers, or really dirty eggs that will not get cleaned.

108
00:06:41,041 --> 00:06:45,280
What we got in here is six cameras that are pointed down

109
00:06:45,380 --> 00:06:47,680
on the eggs as they go through.

110
00:06:47,780 --> 00:06:51,780
Once the camera finds the dirt, pinpoints it,

111
00:06:51,880 --> 00:06:54,980
the computer remembers where it is on the roll,

112
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:59,600
and it'll get rewashed automatically.

113
00:06:59,700 --> 00:07:02,600
Eggs then move across computer-controlled sensors

114
00:07:02,700 --> 00:07:05,000
that check for cracks and weigh the eggs.

115
00:07:05,100 --> 00:07:07,400
The computer will know what size it is,

116
00:07:07,401 --> 00:07:11,600
And then it'll say, OK, you are a medium, you're a large,

117
00:07:11,700 --> 00:07:13,700
you're an X or a jumbo.

118
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:17,040
It'll know where to take it down the line to the carton

119
00:07:17,140 --> 00:07:18,040
where it needs to be packed.

120
00:07:21,340 --> 00:07:23,540
Here, eggs are also graded.

121
00:07:23,640 --> 00:07:26,860
Double A's have the smallest air cells, grade A's slightly

122
00:07:26,960 --> 00:07:33,660
larger, and grade B's larger still, with some discoloration.

123
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:35,400
Finally, workers prepare thousands

124
00:07:35,401 --> 00:07:40,000
cartons of fresh eggs for shipment.

125
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:42,000
Normal shelf life for refrigerated eggs

126
00:07:42,100 --> 00:07:44,000
is three to four weeks.

127
00:07:44,100 --> 00:07:45,340
We're in the cooler right now.

128
00:07:45,440 --> 00:07:47,540
It's about 36 degrees in here.

129
00:07:47,640 --> 00:07:50,240
Where the eggs are stored for a couple of days,

130
00:07:50,340 --> 00:07:54,560
or the day of, they're being shipped out.

131
00:07:54,660 --> 00:07:56,260
With efficient egg production comes

132
00:07:56,360 --> 00:07:58,660
environmental challenges.

133
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:02,000
As manure dries, ammonia forms.

134
00:08:02,100 --> 00:08:04,000
Levels need to be carefully monitored.

135
00:08:04,001 --> 00:08:06,760
Too much ammonia can cause respiratory problems for birds

136
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:09,140
and humans.

137
00:08:09,240 --> 00:08:12,140
Scientists from Iowa State University's Ag Industry

138
00:08:12,240 --> 00:08:15,540
Institute are working with Rose Acre to develop new ways

139
00:08:15,640 --> 00:08:19,240
to control ammonia emissions.

140
00:08:19,340 --> 00:08:22,660
We're inside what we call the mobile air emission monitoring

141
00:08:22,760 --> 00:08:23,560
unit.

142
00:08:23,660 --> 00:08:29,200
We basically have shown in lab that different diets can affect

143
00:08:29,300 --> 00:08:32,560
the ammonia emission from laying hand manure.

144
00:08:32,561 --> 00:08:38,860
Hens are fed a diet of corn, soy meal, vitamins, and minerals.

145
00:08:38,960 --> 00:08:41,760
In labs at Iowa State, variations of that diet

146
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:43,940
are fed to chickens in controlled observation

147
00:08:44,040 --> 00:08:48,540
chambers to determine not only the effects physically,

148
00:08:48,640 --> 00:08:51,080
but psychologically.

149
00:08:51,180 --> 00:08:55,120
A happier bird is probably a healthier bird.

150
00:08:55,220 --> 00:08:57,280
And possibly, they're eating better.

151
00:08:57,380 --> 00:09:01,180
Possibly, they're more feed efficient.

152
00:09:01,181 --> 00:09:03,980
As a result of animal welfare concerns and legislation

153
00:09:04,080 --> 00:09:06,320
in Europe and the United States,

154
00:09:06,420 --> 00:09:08,480
alternatives to traditional cage production

155
00:09:08,580 --> 00:09:11,120
are becoming more common.

156
00:09:11,220 --> 00:09:12,920
Producers like Rose Acre have begun

157
00:09:13,020 --> 00:09:17,220
establishing cage-free facilities.

158
00:09:17,320 --> 00:09:20,840
Smaller companies like Petaluma Farms in Northern California

159
00:09:20,940 --> 00:09:25,640
have been cage-free for more than 20 years.

160
00:09:25,740 --> 00:09:31,180
Petaluma Farms routinely houses between 50,000 and 60,000 hens.

161
00:09:31,181 --> 00:09:33,940
Cage-free farms account for a small part of American egg

162
00:09:34,040 --> 00:09:37,420
production, less than 2%.

163
00:09:37,520 --> 00:09:41,420
The chickens have free choice all day long.

164
00:09:41,520 --> 00:09:44,220
These are the feeders right here.

165
00:09:44,320 --> 00:09:46,420
And then behind me are the nest boxes

166
00:09:46,520 --> 00:09:48,540
that we actually bring in from Europe.

167
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:50,540
The environment of the nest boxes

168
00:09:50,640 --> 00:09:52,740
is a little dark and a little more secluded for the chickens,

169
00:09:52,840 --> 00:09:54,440
so she can feel a little protected when she wants

170
00:09:54,540 --> 00:09:57,740
to lay her egg, as opposed to being out in this open area.

171
00:09:57,840 --> 00:10:00,040
There's actually a belt that runs underneath them

172
00:10:00,041 --> 00:10:02,680
that once a day we kind of shoo all the chickens out

173
00:10:02,780 --> 00:10:04,040
and the eggs roll onto the spelt.

174
00:10:07,240 --> 00:10:10,280
Chicken manure is handled differently at Petaluma Farms,

175
00:10:10,380 --> 00:10:11,880
where it's mixed into layers of litter

176
00:10:11,980 --> 00:10:14,580
on the chicken house floors.

177
00:10:14,680 --> 00:10:16,660
We're using a deep litter situation

178
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:19,520
where we put in six to eight inches of rice holes on the floor

179
00:10:19,620 --> 00:10:22,460
so the birds can root around and dust themselves.

180
00:10:22,560 --> 00:10:24,400
And actually, on the bottom end of it,

181
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,960
as the birds get older, it will eventually compost on its own.

182
00:10:27,961 --> 00:10:30,520
Like their counterparts at caged egg farms,

183
00:10:30,620 --> 00:10:32,400
Petaluma Farms cage-free chickens

184
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:35,900
still need to have their beaks clipped right after birth.

185
00:10:36,000 --> 00:10:37,800
If we left that hook on the end,

186
00:10:37,900 --> 00:10:39,900
they would do real damage to each other when they were

187
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,100
picking at each other, even to preen each other.

188
00:10:43,200 --> 00:10:46,520
Petaluma Farms also raises Rhode Island red chickens

189
00:10:46,620 --> 00:10:49,120
in an environment where roosters mingle with hens,

190
00:10:49,220 --> 00:10:53,620
producing brown-shelled fertile eggs.

191
00:10:53,720 --> 00:10:55,260
Embryos never develop in the eggs

192
00:10:55,261 --> 00:10:58,660
because they're refrigerated within a day of being laid.

193
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:01,800
It's really about the ultimate lifestyle for the chicken

194
00:11:01,900 --> 00:11:04,800
and providing the whole package, so to speak.

195
00:11:04,900 --> 00:11:07,700
The chicken, the rooster, all natural feed.

196
00:11:07,800 --> 00:11:09,800
It's all vegetarian feed.

197
00:11:09,900 --> 00:11:12,820
And this is where it all started in this kind of manner

198
00:11:12,920 --> 00:11:14,820
with these birds.

199
00:11:14,920 --> 00:11:16,620
You might think a white egg comes

200
00:11:16,720 --> 00:11:20,120
from a white chicken and a brown egg from a brown chicken.

201
00:11:20,220 --> 00:11:21,820
Not necessarily.

202
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:24,120
The whiting the brown egg, the difference, actually,

203
00:11:24,121 --> 00:11:25,920
is the earlobe of the chicken.

204
00:11:26,020 --> 00:11:27,420
It's a sex link.

205
00:11:27,520 --> 00:11:30,820
So the earlobe, if it's white, it's going to be a white egg.

206
00:11:30,920 --> 00:11:34,060
And if it's brown, it's going to be a brown egg.

207
00:11:34,160 --> 00:11:37,060
Petaluma farm eggs are cleaned, grated, and prepared

208
00:11:37,160 --> 00:11:39,560
for market, with technology similar to that

209
00:11:39,660 --> 00:11:44,480
used by larger caged farms, but on a much smaller scale.

210
00:11:44,580 --> 00:11:48,420
Market prices range from $3 to $5 per dozen.

211
00:11:48,520 --> 00:11:52,820
But that's still not top dollar for eggs laid in America.

212
00:11:52,821 --> 00:11:55,360
The highest price eggs come from some of the lowest tech

213
00:11:55,460 --> 00:11:57,860
operations.

214
00:11:57,960 --> 00:12:00,360
Eat Well Farm in Dixon, California,

215
00:12:00,460 --> 00:12:03,760
is one of relatively few egg producers that allows chickens

216
00:12:03,860 --> 00:12:06,560
to pasture graze.

217
00:12:06,660 --> 00:12:09,060
British horticulturalist Nigel Walker

218
00:12:09,160 --> 00:12:12,340
moved to the US 20 years ago and purchased land

219
00:12:12,440 --> 00:12:15,280
to produce organic vegetables and farm fresh eggs,

220
00:12:15,380 --> 00:12:17,320
played by chickens who spend most of their days

221
00:12:17,420 --> 00:12:19,980
in the great outdoors.

222
00:12:20,080 --> 00:12:22,120
We don't even call us free range,

223
00:12:22,121 --> 00:12:25,820
Because free range can literally mean a huge barn with 20,000

224
00:12:25,920 --> 00:12:28,160
chickens in with the door open.

225
00:12:28,260 --> 00:12:30,060
The chickens are not used to going out.

226
00:12:30,160 --> 00:12:31,260
They won't go out.

227
00:12:31,360 --> 00:12:33,160
These chickens go out all the time.

228
00:12:33,260 --> 00:12:37,940
We do everything to make it so the chickens have as calm and as

229
00:12:38,040 --> 00:12:40,440
idyllic life as possible.

230
00:12:40,540 --> 00:12:42,880
Nigel purchases his production red chickens

231
00:12:42,980 --> 00:12:45,280
from a local hatchery.

232
00:12:45,380 --> 00:12:47,520
But their beaks are not clipped.

233
00:12:47,620 --> 00:12:49,880
Two chickens get into a fight here.

234
00:12:49,980 --> 00:12:51,580
There's room to run.

235
00:12:51,581 --> 00:12:52,820
They'll peck each other.

236
00:12:52,920 --> 00:12:55,520
It happens here sometimes, but, you know,

237
00:12:55,620 --> 00:12:57,420
people are hen pecked.

238
00:12:57,520 --> 00:12:59,920
There are many hen pecked husbands around,

239
00:13:00,020 --> 00:13:04,220
just as many as there are a few chickens here that are hen pecked.

240
00:13:04,320 --> 00:13:05,820
The hens spent most of their days

241
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:09,340
grazing on natural grasses or eating organic blends

242
00:13:09,440 --> 00:13:11,140
of grain and corn.

243
00:13:11,240 --> 00:13:13,640
We soak the wheat overnight so it makes it more digestible

244
00:13:13,740 --> 00:13:15,580
for the chickens, and they eat a lot of it.

245
00:13:15,680 --> 00:13:20,380
About 40% of their feed is this whole wheat.

246
00:13:20,381 --> 00:13:23,680
The hens lay their eggs and sleep in chicken coops

247
00:13:23,780 --> 00:13:25,980
built on mobile home frames.

248
00:13:26,080 --> 00:13:29,480
There's nest boxes inside where the chickens lay their eggs.

249
00:13:29,580 --> 00:13:31,820
There's roosts where they roost at night.

250
00:13:31,920 --> 00:13:35,720
There's hanging water drinker, nipple drinkers there.

251
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:38,540
So they give some clean water 24-7.

252
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:42,240
And then when the very hot days, when we get above 95 degrees,

253
00:13:42,340 --> 00:13:46,840
we have these misters, these tubes surrounding the houses.

254
00:13:46,940 --> 00:13:48,780
And basically, it's fogs there.

255
00:13:48,781 --> 00:13:50,280
I mean, it's just like, you know,

256
00:13:50,380 --> 00:13:53,320
Southern California patio party.

257
00:13:53,420 --> 00:13:56,120
The mobile chicken coops are part of Nigel's solution

258
00:13:56,220 --> 00:13:58,960
to the manure issue.

259
00:13:59,060 --> 00:14:01,920
After the chickens have grazed a pasture for several months,

260
00:14:02,020 --> 00:14:05,000
dropping manure as they go, the coops and chickens

261
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:08,100
are moved to a new pasture.

262
00:14:08,200 --> 00:14:11,100
The vacated area, having been naturally fertilized,

263
00:14:11,200 --> 00:14:16,240
is then available to grow crops or regenerate pasture grasses.

264
00:14:16,340 --> 00:14:18,640
That works well for us because the chickens

265
00:14:18,641 --> 00:14:20,900
to us is an asset.

266
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,840
Whereas in a big, huge, mega chicken operation,

267
00:14:24,940 --> 00:14:28,080
the chicken is absolutely poison,

268
00:14:28,180 --> 00:14:30,780
and how the hell do we get rid of this damn stuff?

269
00:14:30,880 --> 00:14:32,880
Whereas for us, it's like, oh, my God,

270
00:14:32,980 --> 00:14:34,680
it grows the most fabulous vegetables

271
00:14:34,780 --> 00:14:37,700
after the chickens have moved on.

272
00:14:37,800 --> 00:14:40,500
After a productive life of one to two years,

273
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:45,540
Nigel's chickens also endure what he calls their unhappy day.

274
00:14:45,640 --> 00:14:48,640
When these chickens have finished two years of life,

275
00:14:48,641 --> 00:14:51,680
The third year, they really don't lay enough eggs

276
00:14:51,780 --> 00:14:54,340
to pay for their feed, so they have to go.

277
00:14:54,440 --> 00:14:57,280
You know, we all have to pay our way.

278
00:14:57,380 --> 00:14:59,980
And what happens to them, we have a good demand

279
00:15:00,080 --> 00:15:02,480
for spent chickens, and they go to a slaughterhouse

280
00:15:02,580 --> 00:15:07,200
in Sacramento where they're processed for stewing chickens.

281
00:15:07,300 --> 00:15:10,300
Beatwell Farm eggs are sold only on a co-op basis

282
00:15:10,400 --> 00:15:14,240
with fresh organic produce to private customers.

283
00:15:14,340 --> 00:15:18,440
The eggs cost $8 per dozen, four times the cost

284
00:15:18,441 --> 00:15:20,940
of caged production eggs.

285
00:15:21,040 --> 00:15:24,380
But Nigel says that buyers can taste the difference.

286
00:15:24,480 --> 00:15:28,220
We deliver about 900 boxes of produce every week,

287
00:15:28,320 --> 00:15:30,920
and the customers get the eggs with their boxes.

288
00:15:31,020 --> 00:15:35,160
And we have 200 people waiting to get into the farm.

289
00:15:35,260 --> 00:15:38,800
And it can take anything from five, six, seven months

290
00:15:38,900 --> 00:15:40,300
to get a spot.

291
00:15:40,400 --> 00:15:42,560
And there's never enough eggs.

292
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:45,200
That's just the reality.

293
00:15:45,300 --> 00:15:47,500
Whether a dozen eggs is worth $2

294
00:15:47,501 --> 00:15:51,940
$2 or $8 is a highly subjective choice when only a hen's

295
00:15:51,941 --> 00:15:54,900
environment is considered.

296
00:15:54,901 --> 00:15:58,140
But what about taste and nutrition?

297
00:15:58,141 --> 00:16:00,440
The determining factors might surprise you.

298
00:16:05,760 --> 00:16:08,460
Fresh eggs usually reach markets within two days

299
00:16:08,461 --> 00:16:10,200
of being laid.

300
00:16:10,201 --> 00:16:12,200
Then comes the shopper's dilemma.

301
00:16:12,201 --> 00:16:15,100
So many choices and confusing labels.

302
00:16:15,101 --> 00:16:16,900
What's an egg eater to do?

303
00:16:16,901 --> 00:16:19,940
Let's start with taste and nutritional value.

304
00:16:20,040 --> 00:16:24,980
I know people who pay several dollars more a dozen

305
00:16:25,080 --> 00:16:29,820
for the eggs because they don't want to buy eggs from hens

306
00:16:29,920 --> 00:16:31,380
who were kept in cages.

307
00:16:31,480 --> 00:16:33,480
That's a philosophical decision.

308
00:16:33,580 --> 00:16:36,120
And they have the ability to pay more for it,

309
00:16:36,220 --> 00:16:37,980
and I think that's great.

310
00:16:38,080 --> 00:16:39,960
In terms of nutritional value, there

311
00:16:40,060 --> 00:16:41,520
is no nutritional difference.

312
00:16:41,620 --> 00:16:46,300
So the egg laid by a hen who is maintained in a cage

313
00:16:46,301 --> 00:16:50,240
is going to be same as the nutritional value from an egg

314
00:16:50,340 --> 00:16:53,800
laid by a hen that was on the ground.

315
00:16:53,900 --> 00:16:56,480
What can affect an egg's taste and nutrition, however,

316
00:16:56,580 --> 00:16:59,720
is the hen's diet.

317
00:16:59,820 --> 00:17:03,380
The term organic on a carton can make a difference.

318
00:17:03,480 --> 00:17:08,360
Typically, the birds that are producing organic eggs,

319
00:17:08,460 --> 00:17:13,920
they have to be fed a diet that's made up of grains that

320
00:17:13,921 --> 00:17:17,260
that were also produced organically.

321
00:17:17,261 --> 00:17:19,620
Another significant egg carton term

322
00:17:19,621 --> 00:17:22,520
is omega-3 enhanced.

323
00:17:22,521 --> 00:17:24,860
Hens producing these eggs are fed a diet enriched

324
00:17:24,861 --> 00:17:29,140
with flaxseed, fish oil, or DHA algae

325
00:17:29,141 --> 00:17:32,700
to produce fatty acids that can help prevent heart disease.

326
00:17:32,701 --> 00:17:35,800
If a person really wants to get those omega-3 fatty acids,

327
00:17:35,801 --> 00:17:37,240
this is one way to get them.

328
00:17:37,241 --> 00:17:39,580
So it's really a matter of what are you willing to pay

329
00:17:39,581 --> 00:17:43,240
to get increased omega-3 fatty acids in the diet.

330
00:17:43,241 --> 00:17:45,480
If the choices among eggs sold in cartons

331
00:17:45,580 --> 00:17:49,280
aren't complicated enough, add another decision,

332
00:17:49,380 --> 00:17:52,640
shelled, liquid, or powder.

333
00:17:52,740 --> 00:17:55,480
More than a third of all eggs now leave producers

334
00:17:55,580 --> 00:17:58,580
without shells.

335
00:17:58,680 --> 00:18:01,700
Like its nearby sister farm in Stewart, Iowa,

336
00:18:01,800 --> 00:18:04,300
this Rose Acre facility in Guthrie Center

337
00:18:04,400 --> 00:18:08,300
houses hundreds of thousands of hens.

338
00:18:08,400 --> 00:18:11,100
But most eggs here are processed through the facility's

339
00:18:11,101 --> 00:18:14,340
breaking plan.

340
00:18:14,440 --> 00:18:17,300
As in Stewart, machinery in Guthrie cleans and sorts

341
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:19,440
the eggs automatically.

342
00:18:19,540 --> 00:18:22,340
But checks or cracks are detected not by visual

343
00:18:22,440 --> 00:18:25,640
inspection, but sound waves.

344
00:18:25,740 --> 00:18:29,560
What we have is an acoustical crack detection system.

345
00:18:29,660 --> 00:18:31,860
There's little probes that are tapping the egg,

346
00:18:31,960 --> 00:18:35,360
and it's listening for the different sounds in the egg.

347
00:18:35,460 --> 00:18:38,260
A cracked egg has a different sound.

348
00:18:38,261 --> 00:18:42,060
It'll pick that up and reject that egg that has a crack

349
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:43,560
in the shell.

350
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:45,960
Eggs with imperfections, and many without,

351
00:18:46,060 --> 00:18:48,900
depending on market demand, are cracked and separated

352
00:18:49,000 --> 00:18:50,800
by machine.

353
00:18:50,900 --> 00:18:54,000
It's the same technique a kitchen cook would use,

354
00:18:54,100 --> 00:18:57,420
but the machine does it a bit faster,

355
00:18:57,520 --> 00:19:02,320
at a rate of 28,000 eggs per hour.

356
00:19:02,420 --> 00:19:05,760
Each egg moves into a cracker, where a knife gently cracks

357
00:19:05,761 --> 00:19:08,160
the shell and splits it apart.

358
00:19:08,260 --> 00:19:10,600
The egg drops into a small container with an opening

359
00:19:10,700 --> 00:19:13,260
large enough for the white to separate and drip

360
00:19:13,360 --> 00:19:16,300
into a tray below.

361
00:19:16,400 --> 00:19:20,100
Incredibly, this is old technology.

362
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:22,000
A newer egg breaker just installed

363
00:19:22,100 --> 00:19:27,520
does the same job at the rate of 108,000 eggs per hour.

364
00:19:27,620 --> 00:19:31,260
The shells, rich in calcium, are collected in containers

365
00:19:31,360 --> 00:19:33,760
and sold or given to area farmers

366
00:19:33,761 --> 00:19:35,760
used as crop fertilizer.

367
00:19:35,860 --> 00:19:37,660
They also show promise in their ability

368
00:19:37,760 --> 00:19:41,900
to absorb carbon dioxide in the production of hydrogen.

369
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:45,400
And collagen, harvested from eggshell membranes,

370
00:19:45,500 --> 00:19:47,500
offers numerous commercial applications.

371
00:19:50,820 --> 00:19:54,320
Fresh from the breaker, the whites, yolks, and whole liquid

372
00:19:54,420 --> 00:19:56,320
eggs wait in giant chilled holding

373
00:19:56,420 --> 00:19:59,520
tanks for their next stop in the production line,

374
00:19:59,620 --> 00:20:02,320
pasteurization.

375
00:20:02,321 --> 00:20:04,120
The sweet spot for pasteurization

376
00:20:04,220 --> 00:20:06,960
is only a matter of 10 degrees.

377
00:20:07,060 --> 00:20:10,560
Hot, but not too hot, please.

378
00:20:10,660 --> 00:20:13,200
Normally, we've got to be above 140 degrees

379
00:20:13,300 --> 00:20:16,140
to get a good bacteria kill.

380
00:20:16,240 --> 00:20:19,200
But yeah, we don't want to get above 150 degrees,

381
00:20:19,300 --> 00:20:22,280
or we'll start cooking egg inside that breast.

382
00:20:22,380 --> 00:20:25,040
It could get ugly pretty quick.

383
00:20:25,140 --> 00:20:27,840
Without their natural shells to protect them,

384
00:20:27,940 --> 00:20:31,780
liquid egg products require sanitary processing.

385
00:20:31,781 --> 00:20:35,080
Basically, we're going through some checks to make sure

386
00:20:35,081 --> 00:20:36,620
everything is sterile.

387
00:20:36,621 --> 00:20:40,020
And the first thing that we do is we purchase the bags that

388
00:20:40,021 --> 00:20:44,020
are irradiated already to make sure there's no bacteria growing

389
00:20:44,021 --> 00:20:45,420
inside the bag.

390
00:20:45,421 --> 00:20:49,940
And then from there, the machine is kept sterile with steam.

391
00:20:49,941 --> 00:20:53,940
So the steam keeps the machine hot enough to inhibit bacteria

392
00:20:53,941 --> 00:20:54,940
growth.

393
00:20:54,941 --> 00:20:57,340
The liquid eggs are sent on their way in packaging,

394
00:20:57,341 --> 00:21:01,540
ranging from 160 egg bags to steaming

395
00:21:01,541 --> 00:21:06,780
to stainless steel tanker trucks holding 48,000 pounds of liquid,

396
00:21:06,781 --> 00:21:10,400
more than 400,000 whites or yolks.

397
00:21:10,401 --> 00:21:12,740
The trucks are bound from manufacturers of products

398
00:21:12,741 --> 00:21:16,440
like baked goods or mayonnaise.

399
00:21:16,441 --> 00:21:19,100
The processing of liquid eggs gives them double the shelf life

400
00:21:19,101 --> 00:21:24,180
of refrigerated shelled eggs, nearly two months.

401
00:21:24,181 --> 00:21:26,300
But some customers need even longer periods

402
00:21:26,301 --> 00:21:28,940
of guaranteed freshness.

403
00:21:28,941 --> 00:21:32,180
Enter the powdered egg.

404
00:21:32,181 --> 00:21:34,440
In another state-of-the-art machine,

405
00:21:34,441 --> 00:21:39,180
liquid becomes powder at a rate of 4,000 pounds per hour.

406
00:21:39,181 --> 00:21:42,580
Behind me, what we have is what we call an egg dryer.

407
00:21:42,581 --> 00:21:48,000
What it does is pressurize the liquid egg up to 3,000 psi

408
00:21:48,001 --> 00:21:51,700
and spray that into this box dryer.

409
00:21:51,701 --> 00:21:55,840
Inside the box, it's about 440 degrees.

410
00:21:55,841 --> 00:22:01,120
Within six seconds, the liquid egg is turned into a powder

411
00:22:01,220 --> 00:22:04,220
before it hits the floor.

412
00:22:04,320 --> 00:22:06,420
Outside the dryer, powdered eggs

413
00:22:06,520 --> 00:22:09,620
are sifted and placed into 50-pound bags and boxes

414
00:22:09,720 --> 00:22:11,460
for shipping.

415
00:22:11,560 --> 00:22:14,320
The dried eggs are usually sold to institutions and ice cream

416
00:22:14,420 --> 00:22:17,020
producers.

417
00:22:17,120 --> 00:22:20,600
But liquid product has caught on with many consumers,

418
00:22:20,700 --> 00:22:23,040
especially those who want only egg whites

419
00:22:23,041 --> 00:22:25,440
their cholesterol down.

420
00:22:25,540 --> 00:22:29,580
But are egg yolks really that bad for us?

421
00:22:29,680 --> 00:22:33,380
15 years ago, eggs got a bad name.

422
00:22:33,480 --> 00:22:35,080
In particular, yolks were suddenly

423
00:22:35,180 --> 00:22:38,480
considered as unhealthy as fatty red meats, rich ice creams,

424
00:22:38,580 --> 00:22:40,400
and cheesecake.

425
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:43,800
But that was then, and this is now.

426
00:22:43,900 --> 00:22:45,800
Research scientists in several departments

427
00:22:45,900 --> 00:22:48,540
at Iowa State University have studied the effect

428
00:22:48,640 --> 00:22:52,140
on the human body of eating whole eggs.

429
00:22:52,141 --> 00:22:54,540
They've all come to virtually the same conclusion

430
00:22:54,640 --> 00:22:57,140
as published health recommendations.

431
00:22:57,240 --> 00:22:59,180
The American Heart Association has it set

432
00:22:59,280 --> 00:23:01,680
at a recommendation of four eggs per week.

433
00:23:01,780 --> 00:23:05,580
Other groups will have that ramped up to about one egg a day

434
00:23:05,680 --> 00:23:07,080
or seven a week would be.

435
00:23:07,180 --> 00:23:09,200
So an egg a day is still considered

436
00:23:09,300 --> 00:23:13,700
to be fine for the average, for the normal healthy adult

437
00:23:13,800 --> 00:23:15,800
without any other factors of high cholesterol

438
00:23:15,900 --> 00:23:17,500
or heart disease.

439
00:23:17,600 --> 00:23:19,440
Other tests at Iowa State have involved

440
00:23:19,441 --> 00:23:22,840
feeding rats various sources of protein to determine just how

441
00:23:22,940 --> 00:23:27,140
well egg protein ranks in nutritional value.

442
00:23:27,240 --> 00:23:28,940
The verdict?

443
00:23:29,040 --> 00:23:31,480
You don't need to eat an expensive steak to get a

444
00:23:31,580 --> 00:23:37,000
protein fix when an egg selling for 15 cents will do the job.

445
00:23:37,100 --> 00:23:39,400
Whether it's a bodybuilder trying to put on muscle or

446
00:23:39,500 --> 00:23:42,000
whether it's just an adolescent who's growing through a normal

447
00:23:42,100 --> 00:23:45,940
life cycle, egg protein is still going to be the best protein on

448
00:23:45,941 --> 00:23:52,440
on a per gram basis to grow or to put or to make new muscle.

449
00:23:52,540 --> 00:23:54,780
How's this for proof?

450
00:23:54,880 --> 00:23:58,680
A body like this can be yours, but you'll

451
00:23:58,780 --> 00:24:01,760
have to drink raw egg whites more than you can possibly

452
00:24:01,860 --> 00:24:02,860
imagine.

453
00:24:06,960 --> 00:24:10,540
Meet Eamon Faur, professional bodybuilder.

454
00:24:10,541 --> 00:24:14,540
You see, 23 inch.

455
00:24:14,640 --> 00:24:16,680
To achieve this physique, every day

456
00:24:16,780 --> 00:24:21,680
he repeatedly deadlifts 700 pounds, bench presses 400,

457
00:24:21,780 --> 00:24:24,380
and consumes raw egg whites.

458
00:24:24,480 --> 00:24:36,200
He doesn't stop at 10, not at 20, not even 30, but 40.

459
00:24:40,540 --> 00:24:42,540
And that's not all.

460
00:24:42,640 --> 00:24:45,140
He eats another 40 scrambled.

461
00:24:45,240 --> 00:24:49,180
I need every day between 600 and 700 gram protein.

462
00:24:49,280 --> 00:24:52,580
Like this here, I need every day one bottle

463
00:24:52,680 --> 00:24:55,580
between cook and drink.

464
00:24:55,680 --> 00:24:57,300
Eamon and many other bodybuilders

465
00:24:57,400 --> 00:25:01,400
get their daily protein fix from California-based Eggology.

466
00:25:04,400 --> 00:25:07,860
Containers of 48,000 fresh chilled egg whites

467
00:25:07,861 --> 00:25:11,360
from cage-free chicken-sped organic diets

468
00:25:11,460 --> 00:25:13,700
are delivered to its production facility,

469
00:25:13,800 --> 00:25:16,700
where they're processed for bodybuilders, restaurant chefs,

470
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:18,900
and home cooks.

471
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:21,500
Despite the egg whites having already passed inspection

472
00:25:21,600 --> 00:25:24,400
at their original source, eggology

473
00:25:24,500 --> 00:25:27,640
runs them through another round of safety checks,

474
00:25:27,740 --> 00:25:29,580
particularly because so many buyers

475
00:25:29,680 --> 00:25:32,820
drink the product uncooked.

476
00:25:32,920 --> 00:25:37,860
A USDA inspector continually monitors production.

477
00:25:37,861 --> 00:25:41,660
A proprietary, non-chemical, all-natural process

478
00:25:41,661 --> 00:25:44,340
adds a unique characteristic to these egg whites,

479
00:25:44,960 --> 00:25:48,920
a four-month shelf life, twice that of normal liquid eggs.

480
00:25:49,520 --> 00:25:53,180
Because we're a USDA plant, we have to substantiate anything that's on that label.

481
00:25:53,600 --> 00:25:56,240
So if we're going to claim four months, we have to prove it continuously.

482
00:25:56,720 --> 00:25:59,340
So out of every day's production, we pull samples,

483
00:25:59,840 --> 00:26:03,260
and four months later, we have to open up a sample in front of our inspector.

484
00:26:03,261 --> 00:26:07,900
Eggology has taken the concept of only egg white protein

485
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:13,100
into uncharted territory with yolkless ice creams.

486
00:26:13,200 --> 00:26:15,340
Desserts, they say, are so rich you'd never

487
00:26:15,440 --> 00:26:18,240
guess there was anything missing.

488
00:26:18,340 --> 00:26:22,180
Doggy treats that are actually frozen egg whites

489
00:26:22,280 --> 00:26:25,720
and instant scramblings, four egg whites packaged

490
00:26:25,820 --> 00:26:27,080
in a microwave container.

491
00:26:27,081 --> 00:26:35,580
container, ready to become nuke scrambled in 90 seconds without

492
00:26:35,581 --> 00:26:40,120
even taking the top off. While the bodybuilders and egg

493
00:26:40,121 --> 00:26:43,800
white lovers of California work to create new forms of designer

494
00:26:43,801 --> 00:26:49,740
eggs, back in the egg production capital of Iowa, the art of egg

495
00:26:49,741 --> 00:26:55,100
eating is a bit more traditional. The place to eat eggs in Iowa

496
00:26:55,101 --> 00:27:01,000
City is the Hamburg Inn 2. As a major stop on the Iowa caucus

497
00:27:01,001 --> 00:27:05,400
political highway, the walls are lined with pictures of winners

498
00:27:05,401 --> 00:27:10,940
and losers who came to the inn to get votes and eat eggs.

499
00:27:10,941 --> 00:27:13,560
So what causes the hungry crowds to gather every morning,

500
00:27:13,561 --> 00:27:18,360
starting at sunrise? Here are the secrets of egg-straordinary

501
00:27:18,361 --> 00:27:24,060
egg cooker, Polly Crist. First, you crack the egg on

502
00:27:24,061 --> 00:27:26,060
like this.

503
00:27:26,160 --> 00:27:29,060
You want to do it slowly and carefully.

504
00:27:29,160 --> 00:27:32,060
For Polly, sunny side up means the white is cooked all the way

505
00:27:32,160 --> 00:27:33,700
through, but not the oak.

506
00:27:36,200 --> 00:27:40,820
Now, the trick to turning eggs over without breaking the oaks.

507
00:27:40,920 --> 00:27:43,720
Just crack the egg as I said before.

508
00:27:43,820 --> 00:27:46,820
This time, you want to kind of get them both together

509
00:27:46,920 --> 00:27:49,760
so it's easier to flip.

510
00:27:49,860 --> 00:27:51,220
It's all on the wrist.

511
00:27:51,320 --> 00:27:54,060
The egg never leaves the griddle when it's flipped.

512
00:27:54,061 --> 00:27:56,700
Over easy just cooks the white.

513
00:27:56,800 --> 00:28:00,800
Over medium cooks the yolk just a touch.

514
00:28:00,900 --> 00:28:03,600
What I do is just lightly touch it.

515
00:28:03,700 --> 00:28:06,800
And it's cooked, but not all the way.

516
00:28:06,900 --> 00:28:09,900
It's usually just cooked around the edges like this.

517
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:11,620
And you see that yellow right there?

518
00:28:11,720 --> 00:28:12,820
That's what you want.

519
00:28:12,920 --> 00:28:16,520
That's nice and pretty right there.

520
00:28:16,620 --> 00:28:18,220
The Hamburg, like most restaurants,

521
00:28:18,320 --> 00:28:21,120
serves only grade AA eggs.

522
00:28:21,220 --> 00:28:24,060
They stand up tall on a plate with firm yolks.

523
00:28:24,061 --> 00:28:26,500
and thick whites.

524
00:28:26,600 --> 00:28:31,900
Grade A's are still good, but a bit less high profile.

525
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,400
The thinner whites of a grade B make

526
00:28:34,500 --> 00:28:37,800
for a much flatter fried egg.

527
00:28:37,900 --> 00:28:41,320
And how about a poached egg?

528
00:28:41,420 --> 00:28:43,020
What's my secret?

529
00:28:43,120 --> 00:28:45,220
You want to look for the foam.

530
00:28:45,320 --> 00:28:48,460
As soon as the foam starts bubbling over the top,

531
00:28:48,560 --> 00:28:50,460
you need to turn down the heat.

532
00:28:50,560 --> 00:28:54,060
Now you can see the actual egg still floating in there.

533
00:28:54,061 --> 00:28:56,060
They're not broken.

534
00:28:56,160 --> 00:28:57,060
Perfect.

535
00:28:57,160 --> 00:28:59,460
All the way cooked.

536
00:28:59,560 --> 00:29:02,060
For omelets, the bigger the cooking surface,

537
00:29:02,160 --> 00:29:03,800
the easier it gets.

538
00:29:03,900 --> 00:29:09,900
Just toss, spread, load, and fold.

539
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,020
I've got the Iowa omelet with pancakes.

540
00:29:12,120 --> 00:29:13,220
And Mary, what do you have?

541
00:29:13,320 --> 00:29:15,520
The Iowa omelet with pancakes.

542
00:29:15,620 --> 00:29:19,360
The Hamburgans' signature Iowa omelet is filled with cheese,

543
00:29:19,460 --> 00:29:24,060
ham, and hash browns, not on the side, but folded inside.

544
00:29:24,061 --> 00:29:28,360
An average omelet at the Hamburg contains two grade AA

545
00:29:28,460 --> 00:29:32,460
large eggs and serves one person.

546
00:29:32,560 --> 00:29:36,100
But there is one bird egg that can be made into an omelet to

547
00:29:36,200 --> 00:29:39,100
feed six people or more.

548
00:29:39,200 --> 00:29:40,820
It's not from a chicken.

549
00:29:44,920 --> 00:29:51,160
This is an egg, more specifically an ostrich egg,

550
00:29:51,161 --> 00:29:55,160
containing 24 times the white and yolk of an average chicken egg.

551
00:29:58,160 --> 00:30:01,800
And this is ostrich farm owner Doug Osborne,

552
00:30:01,900 --> 00:30:04,980
giving his daily warnings to ravens eyeing the piles of eggs

553
00:30:05,080 --> 00:30:06,340
that have been laid overnight.

554
00:30:08,580 --> 00:30:10,140
There go the ravens.

555
00:30:10,240 --> 00:30:11,240
There we go.

556
00:30:11,340 --> 00:30:14,280
I'll stay away for an hour or two.

557
00:30:14,380 --> 00:30:18,680
1,500 ostriches currently live on Osborne's OK Corral

558
00:30:18,681 --> 00:30:21,980
Ostrich Farm in Oro Grande, California.

559
00:30:22,080 --> 00:30:24,880
He raises many for meat, which is in great demand

560
00:30:24,980 --> 00:30:28,420
for its flavor, tenderness, and low fat content.

561
00:30:28,520 --> 00:30:30,920
But he is also one of the nation's most prominent

562
00:30:31,020 --> 00:30:33,120
producers of ostrich eggs.

563
00:30:33,220 --> 00:30:35,900
And yes, they are edible.

564
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:38,600
One of the things we believe here on the ostrich farm

565
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:40,440
is that you are what you eat.

566
00:30:40,540 --> 00:30:43,880
And if you are what you eat, the ostrich lives 100 years,

567
00:30:43,980 --> 00:30:47,440
he runs 50 miles an hour, and he mates three times a day.

568
00:30:47,441 --> 00:30:50,680
I mean, what more could you ask for?

569
00:30:50,780 --> 00:30:52,540
For reasons that should be obvious,

570
00:30:52,640 --> 00:30:56,080
the OK Corral is a cage-free farm.

571
00:30:56,180 --> 00:30:58,980
Ostriches standing as high as 9 feet tall

572
00:30:59,080 --> 00:31:02,020
with more devastating leg kick power than a horse

573
00:31:02,120 --> 00:31:05,620
wouldn't have it any other way.

574
00:31:05,720 --> 00:31:07,160
They don't fly.

575
00:31:07,260 --> 00:31:09,460
They don't need to.

576
00:31:09,560 --> 00:31:13,200
You get something about 350, 400 pounds that comes by at 40

577
00:31:13,300 --> 00:31:15,360
miles an hour and hits you, you're not going to have a good

578
00:31:15,460 --> 00:31:16,360
experience.

579
00:31:16,361 --> 00:31:21,660
I think it would be something like the NFL on steroids.

580
00:31:21,760 --> 00:31:24,000
Like chickens, ostriches are followers

581
00:31:24,100 --> 00:31:26,000
when it comes to nesting.

582
00:31:26,100 --> 00:31:29,600
When one decides to lay an egg in a particular location,

583
00:31:29,700 --> 00:31:32,940
others literally stand in line to follow.

584
00:31:33,040 --> 00:31:36,180
We're out here in the southeast corner of the ostrich pen,

585
00:31:36,280 --> 00:31:39,480
and the eggs are laid on a daily basis.

586
00:31:39,580 --> 00:31:43,480
Hens will generally lay about two eggs a week.

587
00:31:43,481 --> 00:31:46,820
And in his pen, we have 450 breeders,

588
00:31:46,821 --> 00:31:49,880
of which about 300 are hens.

589
00:31:49,881 --> 00:31:52,880
So we get hundreds of eggs on a weekly basis,

590
00:31:52,881 --> 00:31:56,420
and this is a typical nest of ostrich eggs here.

591
00:31:56,421 --> 00:31:58,920
And they put little pods of dirt.

592
00:31:58,921 --> 00:32:01,740
They pick up dirt and sprinkle the dirt on top of the eggs,

593
00:32:01,741 --> 00:32:05,140
and I suspect that the reason for that,

594
00:32:05,141 --> 00:32:07,840
in Africa, this would be a good camouflage,

595
00:32:07,841 --> 00:32:10,640
maybe an odor-proofing or something,

596
00:32:10,641 --> 00:32:14,240
or something where other animals wouldn't come along

597
00:32:14,340 --> 00:32:18,580
and want to eat or destroy their eggs.

598
00:32:18,680 --> 00:32:21,420
After gathering the eggs, Osborne candles them

599
00:32:21,520 --> 00:32:24,120
with a low-tech flashlight.

600
00:32:24,220 --> 00:32:25,560
Those showing signs of fertility

601
00:32:25,660 --> 00:32:27,920
are placed in an incubator.

602
00:32:28,020 --> 00:32:31,860
Infertile eggs are prepared for shipment.

603
00:32:31,960 --> 00:32:36,160
Ostrich eggs actually have two markets, the contents for eating

604
00:32:36,260 --> 00:32:40,640
and the shells as collector's items or for artwork.

605
00:32:40,641 --> 00:32:44,440
20 miles from the O.K. Corral farm sits the West Coast home

606
00:32:44,540 --> 00:32:50,280
of the ostrich omelet, the Summit Inn on historic Route 66.

607
00:32:50,380 --> 00:32:51,180
There you go.

608
00:32:51,280 --> 00:32:54,780
I want to show you the way I open the eggs.

609
00:32:54,880 --> 00:32:57,180
When an order comes in for an ostrich omelet,

610
00:32:57,280 --> 00:33:00,500
the first kitchen gadget Chef Aureliano Rios chooses

611
00:33:00,600 --> 00:33:05,200
is a Dremel, the kind of tool a mason uses to drill into concrete.

612
00:33:05,300 --> 00:33:08,140
Because the shells alone sell for $10 or more,

613
00:33:08,141 --> 00:33:11,840
The contents of the egg are emptied through a tiny hole.

614
00:33:11,940 --> 00:33:14,840
Even the most powerful stone dremel bit requires serious

615
00:33:14,940 --> 00:33:18,480
pressure to pierce the hard shell.

616
00:33:18,580 --> 00:33:22,380
An ostrich eggshell is so hard, a 200-pound man could stand

617
00:33:22,480 --> 00:33:25,600
on it, and it wouldn't break.

618
00:33:25,700 --> 00:33:29,900
So how do ostrich chicks ever make their way out?

619
00:33:30,000 --> 00:33:32,940
Over time, approximately 45 days,

620
00:33:33,040 --> 00:33:36,600
thousands of microscopic pores in the shell expand,

621
00:33:36,601 --> 00:33:40,140
making it breakable at just the right time for a baby ostrich

622
00:33:40,240 --> 00:33:43,940
to peck or kick its way out.

623
00:33:44,040 --> 00:33:46,440
The expansion of pores occurs with all eggs,

624
00:33:46,540 --> 00:33:50,240
including those laid by a chicken.

625
00:33:50,340 --> 00:33:54,160
For the ostrich egg chef, once a hole is drilled into a shell,

626
00:33:54,260 --> 00:33:58,060
a vacuum device empties out the white and the yolk.

627
00:33:58,160 --> 00:34:01,700
And the scrambled egg mix finds its way to the griddle,

628
00:34:01,800 --> 00:34:05,300
a very large griddle.

629
00:34:05,301 --> 00:34:08,600
A whole ostrich egg packs over 2,000 calories

630
00:34:08,700 --> 00:34:11,840
and 33 grams of protein, coincidentally

631
00:34:11,940 --> 00:34:16,240
the minimum daily requirement for an average healthy adult.

632
00:34:16,340 --> 00:34:18,140
The cholesterol level?

633
00:34:18,240 --> 00:34:22,060
Approximately 16 times that of a chicken egg.

634
00:34:22,160 --> 00:34:28,260
This omelet also includes cheese and ostrich meat.

635
00:34:28,360 --> 00:34:31,160
The real cooking trick comes when it's time to fold the omelet

636
00:34:31,260 --> 00:34:32,160
for plating.

637
00:34:35,300 --> 00:34:37,300
There you go.

638
00:34:37,400 --> 00:34:40,300
Voila, the only single egg omelet

639
00:34:40,400 --> 00:34:42,100
that won't fit on a large plate.

640
00:34:42,200 --> 00:34:43,300
How about that, huh?

641
00:34:43,400 --> 00:34:45,300
Big omelet.

642
00:34:45,400 --> 00:34:46,740
Oh, wow.

643
00:34:46,840 --> 00:34:48,240
Look at that, baby, would you?

644
00:34:48,340 --> 00:34:49,240
Hi.

645
00:34:49,340 --> 00:34:50,640
That is so good.

646
00:34:50,740 --> 00:34:55,260
Cheddar cheese, avocado, hash browns, sausage.

647
00:34:55,360 --> 00:34:56,760
Mm.

648
00:34:56,860 --> 00:35:00,100
Exotic eggs don't just come from unusual birds.

649
00:35:00,200 --> 00:35:02,600
Some chicken eggs fall into that category

650
00:35:02,700 --> 00:35:04,360
as the result of preparation.

651
00:35:04,361 --> 00:35:07,500
preparation.

652
00:35:07,600 --> 00:35:09,860
Any purple egg may not seem appetizing

653
00:35:09,960 --> 00:35:13,400
to the uninitiated, but at Philippe's restaurant

654
00:35:13,500 --> 00:35:17,400
in Los Angeles, their homemade pickled eggs are so popular,

655
00:35:17,500 --> 00:35:21,640
they need to make a new batch of 600 every three days.

656
00:35:21,740 --> 00:35:24,880
They've been serving them for more than 40 years.

657
00:35:24,980 --> 00:35:27,420
Philippe's is best known for its French dip roast beef

658
00:35:27,520 --> 00:35:30,980
sandwiches, but regular customers frequently add a pickled egg

659
00:35:31,080 --> 00:35:34,360
to their platters.

660
00:35:34,361 --> 00:35:36,600
The eggs originally became popular as bar snacks

661
00:35:36,700 --> 00:35:40,100
in the early 1900s, serving right next to jars

662
00:35:40,200 --> 00:35:42,400
of pickled pig's feet.

663
00:35:42,500 --> 00:35:46,400
The purple color comes from fresh beet juice.

664
00:35:46,500 --> 00:35:49,900
Twice a week, 36 bunches of beets are steamed and then

665
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:51,920
immediately peeled.

666
00:35:52,020 --> 00:35:55,920
After it's peeled, we put them into this slicer.

667
00:35:56,020 --> 00:36:01,020
We slice it, and goes right underneath where all the spices

668
00:36:01,120 --> 00:36:04,360
and the vinegar, and it's ready to use it.

669
00:36:04,361 --> 00:36:07,200
for pickling the eggs.

670
00:36:07,300 --> 00:36:10,040
600 hard-boiled eggs are soaked in the vinegar

671
00:36:10,140 --> 00:36:13,260
and spiced beet juice for three days.

672
00:36:13,360 --> 00:36:15,800
Then they move to the countertop, where they stay fresh up to a

673
00:36:15,900 --> 00:36:17,800
week.

674
00:36:17,900 --> 00:36:20,380
Pickling, in this case, is intended to add flavor more

675
00:36:20,480 --> 00:36:22,280
than preservation.

676
00:36:22,380 --> 00:36:25,240
Some newbies need to be talked into trying one.

677
00:36:25,340 --> 00:36:26,540
I say, OK, try it.

678
00:36:26,640 --> 00:36:29,720
If you don't like it, you're not going to pay for it.

679
00:36:29,820 --> 00:36:31,220
It's on the house.

680
00:36:31,221 --> 00:36:36,220
slice it, and they taste it, and they ask for another one.

681
00:36:36,320 --> 00:36:37,720
When I came here, I came with somebody

682
00:36:37,820 --> 00:36:39,060
who had been coming here for quite a while,

683
00:36:39,160 --> 00:36:41,460
and they said that you got to try them at least once just

684
00:36:41,560 --> 00:36:42,360
to say you did.

685
00:36:42,460 --> 00:36:44,160
I mean, how often do you get to eat a purple egg?

686
00:36:44,260 --> 00:36:46,060
So I went for it, and I really liked it.

687
00:36:46,160 --> 00:36:49,380
I didn't expect that combination of vinegar and sweetness

688
00:36:49,480 --> 00:36:51,780
to be as good as it was.

689
00:36:51,880 --> 00:36:54,680
If you find the color purple intriguing,

690
00:36:54,780 --> 00:36:56,280
how about the color black?

691
00:36:56,380 --> 00:37:01,220
This is an Asian delicacy known as a 1,000-year-old egg.

692
00:37:01,221 --> 00:37:05,360
It's also called a century egg, or simply a preserved egg.

693
00:37:05,361 --> 00:37:07,720
It is not hard-boiled.

694
00:37:07,721 --> 00:37:12,160
It is merely packed in clay, ash, salt, lime, and straw

695
00:37:12,161 --> 00:37:15,260
for up to a month, not really a century.

696
00:37:15,261 --> 00:37:17,160
The cooking is caused by fermentation.

697
00:37:20,180 --> 00:37:22,940
At specialty restaurants like Typhoon in Santa Monica,

698
00:37:22,941 --> 00:37:26,980
California, the egg is simply sliced in half, revealing

699
00:37:26,981 --> 00:37:30,380
a garish green yolk with a smell similar to ammonia.

700
00:37:32,480 --> 00:37:34,080
And it's served over tofu.

701
00:37:35,680 --> 00:37:37,760
Some people say it smells like urine.

702
00:37:39,760 --> 00:37:42,600
But when you taste it, that taste is not there.

703
00:37:42,760 --> 00:37:45,260
That smell that you smell is not the taste you get.

704
00:37:45,940 --> 00:37:47,700
It's a very creamy sort of soft.

705
00:37:49,060 --> 00:37:51,380
It has a yolky flavor to it, but it's very rich.

706
00:37:51,381 --> 00:37:54,140
It's much richer than the normal chicken egg yolk.

707
00:37:54,141 --> 00:37:58,140
Upstairs from Typhoon in the Hump Sushi Bar,

708
00:37:58,240 --> 00:38:01,840
Chef Kiyoshiro Yamamoto creates exotic egg dishes

709
00:38:01,940 --> 00:38:06,920
on a much smaller scale with quail eggs poached

710
00:38:07,020 --> 00:38:11,320
precisely at 140 degrees, so only the yolk cooks,

711
00:38:11,420 --> 00:38:13,420
not the white.

712
00:38:13,520 --> 00:38:17,400
Then he prepares his own spoon-sized omelets.

713
00:38:17,500 --> 00:38:21,240
The sea arching oysters and the king crab,

714
00:38:21,241 --> 00:38:24,840
and a king crab, a Japanese mushroom.

715
00:38:24,940 --> 00:38:28,580
For a sushi chef, quail eggs aren't simply food.

716
00:38:28,680 --> 00:38:32,260
They're miniature works of art.

717
00:38:32,360 --> 00:38:36,660
The simple shape of the egg has inspired artists for centuries.

718
00:38:36,760 --> 00:38:40,420
Today, an Iowa sculptor has become world famous for his own

719
00:38:40,520 --> 00:38:42,760
works of egg art.

720
00:38:42,860 --> 00:38:46,340
How does he create relief carvings like this in a shell

721
00:38:46,440 --> 00:38:48,760
less than 1 32nd of an inch thick?

722
00:38:51,240 --> 00:38:53,240
The perfectly shaped form of the egg

723
00:38:53,340 --> 00:38:56,740
has inspired artists for centuries.

724
00:38:56,840 --> 00:39:02,180
These are Russia's famous Fabergé eggs from the early 1900s,

725
00:39:02,280 --> 00:39:06,680
made from gold, marble, and jewels.

726
00:39:06,780 --> 00:39:11,600
Even simple Easter eggs have been elevated to innovative art.

727
00:39:15,740 --> 00:39:19,140
But few artists have had the patience and steady hand

728
00:39:19,141 --> 00:39:23,240
and steady hand to perform work like eggshell sculptor Gary

729
00:39:23,340 --> 00:39:26,080
Lemaster of Iowa City.

730
00:39:26,180 --> 00:39:29,220
What began as a hobby 30 years ago has evolved

731
00:39:29,320 --> 00:39:30,580
into a life's passion.

732
00:39:35,960 --> 00:39:38,220
Working with an ostrich shell, Gary

733
00:39:38,320 --> 00:39:41,260
is able to carve layers of relief.

734
00:39:41,360 --> 00:39:43,100
Delicate power dental tools allow

735
00:39:43,200 --> 00:39:45,800
him to work within a custom-built case, which

736
00:39:45,801 --> 00:39:48,800
which vacuum shell dust as he carves.

737
00:39:52,800 --> 00:39:56,800
I started just shaping things, taking off the outer layer.

738
00:39:56,801 --> 00:40:01,800
We create the illusion of depth because we're only working with about a,

739
00:40:01,801 --> 00:40:07,800
somewhere between a 32nd of an inch and maybe a 16th of an inch thick shell here.

740
00:40:07,801 --> 00:40:18,800
And so you always point the burr towards what element is going to remain or appear to be higher than the one next to it.

741
00:40:18,801 --> 00:40:25,800
This is a good place to show because we have part of a leaf here that is rolled over on itself.

742
00:40:25,801 --> 00:40:35,800
And instead of being satisfied with just this sort of 90 degree angle here to show the depth,

743
00:40:35,801 --> 00:40:40,200
will actually go in and undercut it, which will create even more

744
00:40:40,300 --> 00:40:44,540
of a shadow and, therefore, more of an illusion of depth.

745
00:40:47,040 --> 00:40:50,040
The natural colors of different eggs give Gary even more

746
00:40:50,140 --> 00:40:54,160
creative opportunities. Beneath the green surface

747
00:40:54,260 --> 00:40:58,660
of an emu eggshell lie multicolor layers.

748
00:40:58,760 --> 00:41:01,700
There's no paint on here, which people traditionally

749
00:41:01,800 --> 00:41:03,860
think there is when they see them.

750
00:41:03,861 --> 00:41:04,561
see them.

751
00:41:04,562 --> 00:41:09,980
When you're carving with an aggressive burr, usually you can get different color

752
00:41:09,981 --> 00:41:16,264
layers from the egg. The white, which is the deepest, and it's just paper thin.

753
00:41:16,265 --> 00:41:16,980
The teal,

754
00:41:17,700 --> 00:41:23,400
which I'm working on now, and I think this is going to eventually go down to the white.

755
00:41:23,401 --> 00:41:26,411
And then of course the dark outer layer.

756
00:41:26,412 --> 00:41:30,400
This is a commission for someone in the great Pacific

757
00:41:30,401 --> 00:41:35,400
whose wife loves dolphins.

758
00:41:35,500 --> 00:41:39,200
Prices for Gary's carved shells range from $100

759
00:41:39,280 --> 00:41:43,340
for simple designs to several thousand for commissioned works.

760
00:41:45,920 --> 00:41:49,280
The lattice or lace-like carvings he calls filigree

761
00:41:49,360 --> 00:41:51,280
often draw the most attention.

762
00:41:54,800 --> 00:42:00,060
You always start at the most fragile

763
00:42:00,061 --> 00:42:03,678
fragile spot because you don't want to end at a fragile spot.

764
00:42:03,679 --> 00:42:05,220
You're almost guaranteeing

765
00:42:05,221 --> 00:42:08,218
that you're going to break the egg.

766
00:42:08,219 --> 00:42:12,500
I'll go back and I'll try to make this even closer

767
00:42:12,501 --> 00:42:14,753
together than it already is.

768
